Fan review complete and saved to `docs/content-analysis/perspective-reviews/Khabib-Nurmagomedov/2026-08-12_231619/fan.md` (SHA verified against context.json before review).

**Verdict:** trust strained, value high, delight clear_hit — **revise** with **2 blockers**, 4 concerns.

**Strongest hit (FAN-H1):** the 2018/2020 memory asymmetry — he remembers and defends the fence-jump fury but can't remember weeping at UFC 254, paid off as "rage is allowed on camera. Weeping is not, so the tape got burned." Both halves are transcript-verified, and no MMA coverage has ever made that connection. The plan-vs-promise distinction (2023 coaching exit reversed vs. 2020 promise to his mother untouched) is a close second — it answers the standing fan objection head-on.

**Blockers, both in the McGregor section — exactly where fan scrutiny is sharpest:**
1. **FAN-R1:** "For 25 minutes of fight time, nothing got in" — the fight lasted 18:03, and judges gave McGregor round 3, one of the two lost rounds the draft's own stat box counts. Wrong twice and internally contradictory.
2. **FAN-R2:** the quoted "almost like my father's age" line appears in neither local transcript (I grepped both), traces only to Tier 3 translated recaps against an explicit research-file instruction not to use quote marks — and the man he actually vaulted at, Dillon Danis, was 25 (born Aug 1993, confirmed via [Wikipedia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dillon_Danis) and [ESPN's brawl coverage](https://www.espn.com/mma/story/_/id/26238580/danis-recounts-details-khabib-mcgregor-postfight-brawl)).

**Highest-priority concern (FAN-C1):** "choked him out in the fourth round" — McGregor tapped and was never unconscious; the draft itself draws the correct went-out distinction for Gaethje, making the looseness stand out to the exact readers who fueled years of "Conor tapped" discourse.
